# Cyrius Field Notes — Language Gotchas (platform & ABI)
# Per-OS preprocessor target flags, struct return by value, variadic fns,
# the fncall6-into-extern-C arg-count ceiling, AArch64 cross-`bl`
# register clobber + immediate-encoding limits, and the agnos
# two-sided ABI syscall-wrapper mirror, plus the aarch64 private-alias
# band and the Darwin AT_* flag divergence.
# Refreshed 2026-08-07 at v6.5.10: added the aarch64 ≥1000 private-alias
# band + the Darwin AT_* divergence from v6.5.7 — the release where ONE
# `vr01_` test finally RUN on ecb / ach turned up seven wrapper defects
# that five targets' worth of compile-only checks could not see.
# Prior refresh 2026-07-27 at v6.4.82: `fncall6_ceiling_into_extern_c`
# carries a premise-check banner — it predates the v6.4.64 Win64
# stack-arg codegen fix, and PURE cyrius has no arity limit at all
# (5-20 args verified on real hardware). A rule that tells you to
# restructure code around codegen is a bug report, not a rule.
# 8 entries.

[[entries]]
name = "per_os_target_flags_win_vs_linux"
description = "v5.5.0 introduced CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN vs CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX preprocessor flags for per-OS stdlib selection"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.5.0 ships `cycc_win` (the Windows cross-compiler) alongside
`cycc` and `cycc_aarch64`. The three cross-entries now predefine
a target-OS flag so stdlib selectors can include the right peer:

    cycc            predefines  CYRIUS_ARCH_X86     + CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX
    cycc_aarch64    predefines  CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64 + CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX
    cycc_win        predefines  CYRIUS_ARCH_X86     + CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN

The two target-OS flags are mutually exclusive. Selectors use
paired `#ifdef` guards rather than `#ifdef` + `#else` (cyrius's
preprocessor has no `#else` / `#ifndef` yet):

    #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN
    include "lib/syscalls_windows.cyr"
    #endif
    #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86
    include "lib/syscalls_x86_64_linux.cyr"
    #endif
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64
    include "lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr"
    #endif
    #endif

Only one of the three inner blocks runs per compile. Adding a
new target OS (e.g. BSD) is another predefined flag + another
top-level block in the selector.

When writing stdlib modules that have OS-specific behaviour, use
the same pattern:
    lib/foo.cyr          public dispatch (selector)
    lib/foo_linux.cyr    Linux peer
    lib/foo_windows.cyr  Windows peer

[[entries]]
name = "struct_return_by_value_5_5_36"
description = "`fn make(): Point { ... return p; }` compiles — struct return is native on both SysV and Win64"
category = "cyrius"
---
Pre-v5.5.36 callers had to pass `&Point` out-params; the
compiler didn't wire struct-by-value return for >8B types.
v5.5.36 closed it:

    fn make_point(x: int, y: int): Point {
        stack var p: Point;
        p.x = x;
        p.y = y;
        return p;
    }

    fn caller() {
        var got: Point = make_point(3, 4);  // deep copy
    }

ABI plumbing (automatic):
 - SysV: hidden retptr in RDI (first arg).
 - Win64: hidden retptr in RCX (first arg).
 - Callee memcpys the local into *retptr before RET.

The `var got: Point = make_point(...)` form on the LHS is also
new in v5.5.36 — parser recognises struct-returning call on the
RHS and emits the retptr setup.

Use return-by-value for small packed values (Point, Range,
Result). Long-lived structs should still live on the heap.

[[entries]]
name = "variadic_fn_5_5_36"
description = "`fn printf(fmt: str, ...)` syntax lands in v5.5.36 (Phase 3-min); use lib/varargs.cyr inside the body"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.5.36 added `...` as the variadic-parameter token:

    fn printf(fmt: str, ...) {
        // use lib/varargs.cyr va_start / va_arg / va_end
    }

`...` is TOK=112, only valid as the last parameter. The compiler
sets a GFVA flag on the callee so call sites know to handle
variadic ABI quirks:
 - Win64 MSDN rule: float args to a variadic callee go into
   BOTH the XMM register file AND the RCX/RDX/R8/R9 GPR slots.
   The compiler emits the duplication at the call site; you
   don't write it.
 - SysV passes float args in XMM0-7 as usual; RAX on the call
   holds the number of XMM regs used, per SysV §3.5.7.

Phase 3-min (shipped v5.5.36) covers primitives-through-varargs.
Phase 3-full (future) expands va_arg to structs-by-value and
nested varargs — for now, if you need those shapes, pass a
pointer or a single `u64` and unpack.

[[entries]]
name = "fncall6_ceiling_into_extern_c"
description = "Cyrius fns that internally fncall* into wgpu-native (or any extern-C) were reported to segfault at 7+ params; pure Cyrius has NO arity limit (5-20 verified v6.4.64). UNVERIFIED since v6.4.64, which fixed a Win64 stack-arg codegen P0 in the same shape — premise-check before adopting the struct-pointer workaround."
category = "cyrius"
---
**PREMISE-CHECK BEFORE ADOPTING THE WORKAROUND (added at the
v6.4.82 closeout).** This entry has not been re-verified since
**v6.4.64**, which fixed a real Win64 codegen P0 in exactly this
shape: `ECALLPOPS`'s PE branch shuttled stack args through a fixed
5-register table with no code path past `nextra == 5`, so a call at
10+ args both mis-popped the register args and emitted NO stack-arg
writes — silently corrupting argument 1. SysV had had the same class
fixed at v5.6.24 / v6.0.57. It is entirely possible that some or all
of the crash described below was that compiler bug rather than an
inherent FFI boundary. Reproduce the 7-arg crash on your current
toolchain before restructuring your API around it — and if it
reproduces, that is a compiler bug to FILE, not a rule to code
around. (This is the language repo: when the compiler can't compile
valid cyrius, fix the compiler.)

---

A Cyrius fn signature CAN take 7+ args without issue when the
body is pure Cyrius — **there is no cyrius arity limit** (5-20 args
verified on real Windows / Linux / aarch64 at v6.4.64). The claim
below is specifically about the extern-C boundary: the moment the
body contains a `fncall*`
into wgpu-native (or any extern-C function table), the whole
signature must stay at ≤ 6 args or the call segfaults at
runtime.

Example shape that crashes:

```cyrius
fn _backend_wgpu_some_op(ctx, a, b, c, d, e, f) {  # 7 args
    fncall6(slot, a, b, c, d, e, f);                # crash
}
```

Same fn at 6 args works. Same fn at 7 args without the inner
fncall* works (pure Cyrius all the way down).

**Why.** The Cyrius calling convention for fns that internally
make C-ABI calls passes args through registers in a way that
doesn't fully line up with the System V x86_64 ABI when the
caller has 7+ args of its own. The first 6 params land in
rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx/r8/r9 cleanly; the 7th would go on the stack,
and the stack alignment / spill protocol differs from how
wgpu-native expects to be called. Result: register clobber that
manifests as a segfault inside the wgpu fn, not at the call
site.

**The fix.** Pack 7+ args into a struct pointer:

```cyrius
fn _backend_wgpu_some_op(ctx, args_ptr) {
    var a = load64(args_ptr + 0);
    var b = load64(args_ptr + 8);
    # ... unpack rest ...
    fncall6(slot, a, b, c, d, e, f);
}
```

Caller side allocates a struct, fills the fields, passes the
ptr. This is the pattern mabda uses for `wgpu_*` ops with 7+
inputs (e.g., `wgpu_command_encoder_copy_buffer_to_buffer`).

**When it doesn't apply.** Pure Cyrius fns with no inner
fncall* into extern-C — those take 12+ args fine. mabda's
internal helpers like `native_pm4_build_render_clear_triangle`
take 6 args and would work at 12 if needed.

**Cross-pollination.** Same constraint will apply to any
Cyrius project that builds a function-table-of-extern-C-fns
abstraction: `samvada` (sd_bus C shim), future
`vidya_logind`-style packages, anything wrapping libc beyond
the stdlib. Document at the boundary; don't trial-and-error.

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_callee_saved_and_imm_limits"
description = "AArch64 cross-`bl` clobbers x0–x18 (caller-saved); `cmp xN, #imm` is 12-bit unsigned, `mov xN, #imm` is 16-bit — larger values need ldr literal-pool"
category = "cyrius"
---
THREE CONJOINED AArch64 ABI / encoding gotchas, all surfaced
during the v2.3.3 → v2.3.4 vidya backfill. Different in
mechanism, identical in symptom: the assembler accepts the
source, the binary runs, the output is wrong.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
1. Caller-saved vs callee-saved across `bl`
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

AArch64 AAPCS64 reserves 32 GP registers split into roles. The
ones that matter for hand-written asm calling helper fns:

    x0–x7    — argument / return value (caller-saved)
    x8       — indirect result location (caller-saved)
    x9–x15   — temporaries (caller-saved)
    x16, x17 — intra-procedure-call scratch (caller-saved)
    x18      — platform register / TLS scratch (caller-saved)
    x19–x28  — callee-saved (helper preserves on entry, restores on exit)
    x29      — frame pointer
    x30      — link register (return address)

Any value held in x0–x18 across a `bl` to a helper is **assumed
gone** unless the caller spilled it. The helper is free to
clobber every one of those registers — and stdlib helpers
DO clobber them, even when their visible job is "compute one
small thing."

Two rescue patterns:

  (a) Cache loop state in callee-saved registers x19–x28:

      stp x29, x30, [sp, #-16]!
      stp x19, x20, [sp, #-16]!     # save callee-saveds before use
      mov x19, x0                    # cache loop counter
      mov x20, x1                    # cache base ptr
    .L1:
      mov x0, x19                    # restore from cache
      bl  helper                     # x0–x18 clobbered, x19/x20 fine
      add x19, x19, #1
      cmp x19, #100
      b.lt .L1
      ldp x19, x20, [sp], #16
      ldp x29, x30, [sp], #16
      ret

      The `stp/ldp` pair before/after the loop is mandatory —
      the helper expects to find x19/x20 unchanged when *it*
      returns to ITS caller, which is your function returning
      to ITS caller. Forgetting the spill turns a clean local
      bug into a callee-clobber that surfaces several frames
      up.

  (b) Recompute after each call, don't cache:

      mov x0, x9                     # x9 holds idx
      bl  helper                     # x9 may be clobbered now
      ldr x9, [sp, #16]              # reload idx from frame
      add x9, x9, #1
      str x9, [sp, #16]
      ...

      Slower (one ldr/str per iteration vs free register reads)
      but doesn't require setting up a callee-saved spill at
      function entry. Use when the loop is shallow or the
      cached value is only read once per call.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.3  grid_pathfinding/asm_x86_64.s — A* helper called
          `manhattan` via bl; the outer loop's row/col indices
          lived in caller-saved regs and got clobbered. Same
          symptom on AArch64 sibling. Caught by the BFS-vs-A*
          path-length agreement test.
  v2.3.3  maze_generation/asm_x86_64.s — `idx` register
          clobbered across the PCG `rng_next` bl. PCG output
          sequence diverged from the reference seed=42 trace.
  v2.3.4  btree_indexing/asm_aarch64.s — node-walk loop cached
          parent pointer in x10 across `bl key_cmp`. Tree
          walk corrupted on first split. 3rd surfacing.
  v2.3.4  sql_parsing/asm_aarch64.s — token-stream loop cached
          token index in x12. SELECT validator silently
          accepted malformed input. 4th surfacing.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
2. `cmp xN, #imm` is 12-bit unsigned
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The immediate field of `cmp xN, #imm` (and `subs xzr, xN, #imm`,
which is what `cmp` is) is 12 bits unsigned, with an optional
LSL #12 shift. So the legal range without shift is 0–4095, and
with the shift it's 4096–16777215 in steps of 4096 — useful for
page boundaries, useless for arbitrary numbers.

    cmp x0, #4095          # OK
    cmp x0, #4096          # ERROR: immediate out of range
    cmp x0, #4166          # ERROR (DT_US/4 = 4166)

CORRECT SHAPE — load the immediate into a temp register, then
compare register-to-register:

    ldr x16, =4166         # literal pool (assembler emits an
                           # ldr from a PC-relative pool slot)
    cmp x0, x16            # OK — register form has no immediate

`x16` (IP0) is the canonical scratch register for this — it's
caller-saved AND reserved for intra-procedure-call use, so the
linker won't repurpose it for veneer insertion.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.3  game_loop_architecture/asm_aarch64.s — fixed-timestep
          accumulator compared against `DT_US/4 = 4166`. The
          assembler error was clear ("immediate out of range")
          but the fix wasn't obvious — the natural reflex is
          "use shift," which doesn't apply here.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
3. `mov xN, #imm` is 16-bit
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`mov xN, #imm` is a synthetic instruction that the assembler
expands to `movz` (move with zero) or `movn` (move with NOT).
Both encodings carry a 16-bit immediate plus a 2-bit shift
selector (LSL by 0 / 16 / 32 / 48). So a single `mov` covers
any 16-bit value, plus 16-bit values shifted to any quarter of
the 64-bit word.

For a fully arbitrary 64-bit constant, the assembler chains
`movz` + up to three `movk` (move with keep) instructions,
each filling one 16-bit slot. Most assemblers do this expansion
silently when you write `mov xN, #imm` — but the failure mode
when the value can't be expressed cleanly is, again, "immediate
out of range."

    mov x0, #65535         # OK — single movz
    mov x0, #65536         # OK — single movz, LSL #16
    mov x0, #100000        # error or silent-wrong (depends on as)
    mov x0, #1442695040888963407   # error — needs 4-instr chain

CORRECT SHAPE — same rescue as `cmp`: literal pool + ldr.

    ldr x0, =1442695040888963407   # PCG64 default-stream
                                   # increment; one instruction,
                                   # one 8-byte pool slot

The assembler chooses between mov-chain (no pool, 4 instructions,
no memory access) and ldr-from-pool (1 instruction, 1 memory
load) based on the constant's shape. For PCG-style 64-bit
multipliers and ChaCha-style state words, ldr-from-pool always
wins.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.3  maze_generation/asm_aarch64.s — PCG multiplier
          0x5851F42D4C957F2D loaded via mov, only the bottom
          16 bits actually landed. Maze generation produced
          deterministic output, but the wrong deterministic
          output. Caught by the cross-language byte-parity
          assertion (`cells[0]=13, cells[27]=12, cells[63]=6`)
          when the AArch64 port disagreed with all 9 other
          ports.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
The pattern across all three
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

AArch64's encoding-tight ISA + the AAPCS64 split means writing
asm for it differs from x86_64 in three predictable ways:

  - You can't dump arbitrary 64-bit constants into instructions.
    Use the literal pool (`ldr xN, =value`) by reflex when the
    constant exceeds 16 bits.
  - You can't park values in caller-saved regs across a `bl`.
    Decide once per function: callee-saved (set up a spill at
    entry, pay one stp + one ldp) OR memory (one ldr/str per
    use, slower but no setup).
  - The assembler error messages are mostly accurate, but they
    don't suggest the literal-pool fix. The reflex is to add a
    shift; the literal pool is the right answer ~90% of the
    time.

x86_64 hides all three: arbitrary 64-bit immediates fit in
`movabs`, caller-saved registers are fewer (rax, rcx, rdx, rsi,
rdi, r8–r11), so the surface area of "what can I keep across a
call" is smaller and more obvious. Porting an x86_64 asm
example to aarch64 always surfaces at least one of these three.

[[entries]]
name = "agnos_two_sided_abi_mirror"
description = "An `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` syscall-wrapper band is byte-identical on every non-agnos target; verify agnos-only wrappers via agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh + a call-probe. The a4=r10 5-arg syscall form rides r10 (push→pop)."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.4.2** — the agnos audio syscall band (`sys_snd_*`, #64–#69) landed as the ring-3 half of a two-sided ABI (kernel HDA handlers land in parallel on the agnos arm). The pattern for adding an agnos syscall wrapper, and why it's low-risk to the cross-OS self-host:

THE TWO-SIDED MIRROR. `lib/syscalls_x86_64_agnos.cyr` is a STANDALONE faithful mirror of `agnos/docs/development/agnos-userland-abi.md` — it does NOT include `syscalls_linux_common.cyr`. agnos has its own numbers, exit=0 (not Linux 60), AO_* open flags (not Linux O_*), and the explicit-length invariant (every path arg carries its length — no NUL assumption). The register convention IS the same as x86_64-Linux (rax=num, rdi/rsi/rdx/r10 = a1..a4), so the `syscall` instruction EMIT is unchanged — only the NUMBERS differ. RE-FREEZE RULE (§5): the kernel doc is canonical; this file mirrors it. If a number/signature changes in the doc, re-freeze here.

BYTE-IDENTICAL EVERYWHERE ELSE. The wrappers are `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS`-gated (the flag is predefined only for the agnos emit target, `CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS=1`; `src/main.cyr` reads it early). So they're #ifdef'd OUT of every non-agnos build → cycc self-hosts byte-identical on Linux/macOS/Windows/aarch64, and seed → cybs → cycc stays byte-identical. A pure-additive agnos wrapper band is NOT a compiler change.

HOW TO VERIFY AGNOS-ONLY CODE (a Linux-hosted cycc can't run it). Two gates, both host-side: (1) the standing `scripts/agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh` (9 sub-checks → a valid agnos ELF) must stay green; (2) a CALL-PROBE — a tiny program that calls every new wrapper, compiled with `build/cyrius build --agnos`, must produce a valid agnos ELF (rc=0, no reachable-undefined fn) and emit `syscall` with the right `eax` numbers. Runtime (HDA → ALC897 for audio) validates QEMU-side on the agnos arm, not here.

THE a4=r10 5-ARG FORM. agnos's 4-arg syscalls pass the 4th arg in r10 (§1a). In Cyrius source that's a 5-arg `syscall(N, a1, a2, a3, a4)` call — e.g. `sys_snd_write_nb(slot,buf,frames)` is `syscall(SYS_SND_WRITE, slot, buf, frames, 1)` where the trailing `1` = O_NONBLOCK bit0. The compiler passes that 4th syscall arg through **push → pop %r10**, so the non-blocking wrapper shares the exact instruction shape of the blocking form (`sys_symlink#63` uses the same 5-arg form) — no ABI drift between them.

The band: `SND_OPEN=64, SND_CONFIG=65, SND_WRITE=66, SND_CLOSE=67, SND_DRAIN=68, SND_AVAIL=69`; seven wrappers (write + write_nb share #66). Frozen ABI is a single 48000 Hz / 16-bit / stereo S16-interleaved output stream, slot 0..3, and #65–#69 reject a slot not owned by the caller (-1).


[[entries]]
name = "sys_exit_is_per_thread_use_sys_exit_group"
description = "`sys_exit` is exit(2) and ends only the CALLING THREAD — a threaded program's epilogue must call `sys_exit_group` (v6.5.6, all 3 peer families). SYS_EXIT_GROUP is absent on agnos, unrouted on PE, and was an untranslated SIGSYS on macOS-x86."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.6** — filed by agnosai after a ported server would not terminate. `sys_exit` wraps **exit(2)**, which ends only the calling thread. In a single-threaded program that ends the process, so the idiomatic epilogue looked correct for years:

    var r = main();
    syscall(SYS_EXIT, r);       # ends ONE thread

The moment the program spawns its first worker — `thread_create`, a pooled server, any `chan_*` fan-out — the same line stops terminating the process: the main thread dies, the workers keep running, and it **hangs with no diagnostic**. Measured, one token apart: `syscall(SYS_EXIT, r)` → timed out (124); `sys_exit_group(r)` → 42.

WHY IT SURVIVED SO LONG. `cyrius init` shipped that epilogue in **seven** templates, so it was the default a consumer copied and then stopped looking at — and the failure only appears long after the epilogue was written, when the program grows a thread. The bug is in the *interaction*, not the line, which is why nothing flagged it.

WHY THE WRAPPER NEEDED THREE DEFINITIONS, NOT ONE. `lib/syscalls_linux_common.cyr` is included by only three peers — Linux-x86, Linux-aarch64, and **macOS-x86** (`syscalls_macos.cyr:306`). Windows and agnos are STANDALONE. A single definition there would have left `sys_exit_group` undefined on two targets, so a portable epilogue could not name it:

    syscalls_linux_common  -> SYS_EXIT_GROUP   231 x86 / 94 aarch64
    syscalls_windows       -> SYS_EXIT         ExitProcess ALREADY ends every thread;
                                               231 has no PE reroute and would emit a
                                               raw 0F 05 (illegal instruction)
    syscalls_x86_64_agnos  -> SYS_EXIT         agnos defines no exit_group; #0 tears
                                               down the whole proc

macOS-arm64 needs nothing: `lib/syscalls.cyr` routes it to the **aarch64** peer, so it resolves 94, and `src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr` already maps `94 -> BSD 1`.

THE HOLE THIS EXPOSED. macOS-**x86** resolves `SYS_EXIT_GROUP` to 231 and `EMACHO_SYSXLAT` had **no 231 entry**, so the raw number reached Darwin *unclassed* (class 0 = `SYSCALL_CLASS_NONE`) and the kernel killed the process with SIGSYS. It was silent at compile time as well: the "syscall not routed" warning is gated on `_TARGET_MACHO == 2`, i.e. **arm64 only**, so an x86-Mach-O build compiled clean and died at runtime. Fixed with one table entry (`231 -> 0x2000001`) beside the existing `60 -> 1`. The general lesson: a constant being *defined* on a target does not mean it is *routed* there — for Mach-O and PE, grep the translation table, not the enum.

Gate: `tests/exit_group_wrapper.sh`. Its axis 2 asserts the OLD epilogue **still hangs** — without that, axis 1 would silently stop proving anything the day the premise changed.

[[entries]]
name = "raw_syscall_numbers_on_aarch64_and_the_darwin_at_flag_divergence"
description = "On aarch64, a raw `syscall(N, …)` is NOT the number the kernel sees — ESYSXLAT rewrites a band of x86-compat numbers, so the native `chdir` (49) ran `bind` and the native `fchownat` (54) ran `setsockopt`. v6.5.7 opened a cyrius-private alias band at **≥1000** (`1000 + native number`) for the cases where BOTH the native and the x86 number are already owned. Separately, the `AT_*` FLAGS diverge on Darwin — only `AT_FDCWD` had ever been corrected — so a Linux-valued flag reaching Darwin is simply invalid: EINVAL, forever, silently."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.7.** Two distinct traps, found the same way: by adding ONE
`vr01_syscall_wrappers.tcyr` to the release gate's cross-OS leg so the
wrappers were finally RUN on ecb / ach instead of only compiled.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
1. A raw `syscall(N, …)` on aarch64 is not the number the kernel sees
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`ESYSXLAT` (`src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr`) rewrites a chain of
x86-compat source numbers so that 51 ecosystem repos emitting raw
`syscall(54, …)` and friends keep working on ARM. The consequence is
that some **aarch64-NATIVE** numbers are already spoken for as x86
source numbers, and a native-numbered call is silently re-aimed:

    source 49  → 200   # x86-compat bind      — so native chdir(49)     ran bind
    source 54  → 208   # x86-compat setsockopt— so native fchownat(54)  ran setsockopt

The documented escape has always been "use the OTHER architecture's
number plus a pure-renumber ESYSXLAT row". For these two there was no
number left to borrow: x86's 80 is this peer's own `SYS_FSTAT`, and
x86's 260 is its own `SYS_WAIT4` (native, must pass through
untranslated).

THE ANSWER — **the private alias band.** Source numbers **≥1000** are
cyrius-private aliases no OS will ever mint (Linux is in the 400s and
grows ~5/yr; Darwin is routed separately), renumbered to their true
target by ESYSXLAT. Convention is `1000 + the native aarch64 number`,
so the alias documents itself:

    SYS_FCHOWNAT = 1054     # → 54 on aarch64-Linux, → 468 on Darwin
    SYS_CHDIR    = 1049     # → 49 on aarch64-Linux, → 12  on Darwin

⛔ **Adding an alias needs THREE edits, not one**: an ESYSXLAT arm on
the aarch64-**Linux** branch, one on the **macho** branch, AND a
`_macho_arm_routes` row — because macOS-arm64 resolves this same peer.
Two out of three is a stale-`x16` garbage call.

⚠ **The aarch64-Linux arm MUST sit LAST in its chain.** It produces
`x8 = 54`, and the `setsockopt` entry earlier in that chain compares
against 54 and would re-catch it — every `fchownat` silently reissued
as `setsockopt`. Mutation-verified: the misordered build returns
`ENOPROTOOPT`. Same ordering trap already documented on flock / poll /
epoll_wait.

PRACTICAL RULE FOR CONSUMERS: **do not emit raw `syscall(N, …)` with a
number you read off a Linux man page.** Use the named `SYS_*` constant
from the peer, or a `sys_*` wrapper. The constant is the only thing
that knows about the translation chain — and note the failure mode is
never a compile error and never a signal, just a different syscall
returning a plausible errno.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
2. The `AT_*` flags diverge on Darwin — and only the dirfd was fixed
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`AT_FDCWD` was corrected to **-2** on macOS when ports found it. The
FLAGS were left Linux-valued for another two-and-a-half minors. Live
values (`lib/syscalls_linux_common.cyr`, read off Darwin's
`sys/fcntl.h` on ecb):

    constant              Darwin   Linux
    AT_FDCWD                  -2    -100
    AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW     0x20   0x100
    AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW       0x40   0x400
    AT_REMOVEDIR            0x80   0x200
    AT_EMPTY_PATH          (none)  0x1000   ← Darwin has NO equivalent

The at-family entries are **pure renumbers** — they pass their flags
straight through — so a Linux value on Darwin is not "wrong-ish", it is
an invalid flag: EINVAL, no warning, no fault, forever. Measured on
real ecb: `xrmdir` had **never** worked on macOS-arm64 since it shipped
at v6.5.2, and `sys_fchownat` returned -22 the first time it was ever
run there.

⭐ **THE THROUGH-LINE, worth more than either fact.** Five of the seven
defects that release were **half-fixes that stopped at the first
symptom**: `AT_FDCWD` without its flags; `STAT_SIZE` corrected without
its sibling offsets (so every `STAT_MODE` test on Intel-Mac was
silently false, reading the low half of `st_ino`); `unlink` mapped
without `rmdir`, which is the SAME syscall distinguished only by
`AT_REMOVEDIR`; three of four link syscalls mapped. Each looked
finished because the thing that prompted it started working. When you
fix one constant or one route in a family, fix the family.

AND THE COMPANION RULE, from the entry above: **a constant being
DEFINED on a target does not mean it is ROUTED there.** For Mach-O and
PE, grep the translation table, not the enum. The "syscall not routed"
warning is gated on `_TARGET_MACHO == 2` — arm64 only — so an
x86-Mach-O build compiles clean and dies at runtime.
